<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Winterthur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Winterthur is a city in the Swiss canton of Zürich, north-east of the city of Zürich, and the canton's second largest town. In 919 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-ii-duke-of-swabia">Burchard II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-of-burgundy">Rudolf II of Upper Burgundy</a> near the town. Held by the counts of Kyburg and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, it passed to Zürich in 1467 and grew into an important centre of engineering and textiles in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winterthur.jpg" alt="Winterthur" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JoachimKohler-HB · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_auf_die_Winterthurer_Altstadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Winterthur lies at an elevation of 439 metres in a basin south and east of the river Töss, about 20 km north-east of Zurich. The small river Eulach runs through the middle of the town to meet the Töss at its western edge, which is why the town is colloquially called Eulachstadt. It stands at the junction of seven roads, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> the army that held it commanded access to most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the crossings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Vitudurum was a Roman vicus in what is now Oberwinterthur from the first century BCE to the third century CE, and was fortified as a castrum at the end of the third century, apparently against the first Alamannic invasion. There was an Alamannic settlement on the site in the seventh century. The counts of Winterthur, a cadet branch of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bregenz">Bregenz</a>, built Kyburg castle in the tenth century. After they became extinct in 1053, the castle passed to the counts of Dillingen, and Hartmann III of Dillingen founded the city in 1180, shortly before his death. It was ruled by a cadet line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-kyburg">House of Kyburg</a> until 1263.</p><p>When the counts of Kyburg died out in the male line in 1263, Winterthur passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a>, who granted it city rights in 1264. It was reichsfrei, subject only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, from 1415 to 1442, but lost this freedom in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-zurich-war">Old Zürich War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, needing money, sold it to the city of Zurich in 1467. Under Zurich its economic freedom was restricted and it lost many market rights, until Napoleonic troops took the town in 1798. On 27 May 1799 French and Habsburg forces under Friedrich, Baron von Hotze fought the Battle of Winterthur, in which the Austrians sustained an 11-hour assault and so consolidated three Austrian forces.</p><p>In the 19th century companies such as Sulzer, Rieter and SLM built large industrial plants. The town suffered from guaranteeing loans to the National Railway of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>: it sold its shares in 1878 and was in great difficulties from 1881 to 1885, helped by loans from the cantonal and federal governments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> hit it hard, since sixty per cent of employees worked in the machine industry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The church of St. Laurenz in the city centre dates from 1264 and the town hall from 1781. The Orchester Musikkollegium Winterthur is the oldest orchestra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and between 1922 and 1950 the patron Werner Reinhart and the conductor Hermann Scherchen shaped the city's musical life. Its museums include the Oskar Reinhart collections, and in 1989 the city received the Wakker Prize for the preservation of its architectural heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Winterthur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rural territory, built up in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries by purchase, pledge and conquest from indebted nobles and the Habsburgs, included the county of Kyburg, pledged to Zürich in 1424, and the town of <b>Winterthur</b>, acquired in 1467.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The castle of Kyburg near <b>Winterthur</b> belonged to the counts of Winterthur in the early 11th century; in 1027 it was besieged by Emperor Conrad II during the revolt of Duke Ernest II of Swabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-kyburg">County of Kyburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Rudolf II was defeated by Swabia at <b>Winterthur</b> in 919, then reached for the Italian crown in the 920s before trading those claims for Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-burgundy">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</a></li></ul></section>
